  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Both bundle a phone number with your data, but they solve it differently. <strong>ByteSIM</strong> gives you a real local number in a handful of countries, at the cost of ID verification. <strong>AloSIM</strong> gives you a free virtual second line through Hushed, in any country, with no ID check at all. Which one you want depends on whether you need a number a local business or bank will actually dial, or just a number you can hand out without giving up your real one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Real Local Number vs. a Virtual One</h2>
  <p>
    ByteSIM sells plans with a genuine local phone number in select countries (the US, Canada, UK, France, Spain, Australia, Vietnam, South Korea, and Thailand), bundled with local calls, texts, hotspot sharing, and a local IP. Getting that number means uploading a passport photo and a selfie for identity verification, standard practice for actually being issued a number under local telecom rules, but a real extra step compared to buying data alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    AloSIM's phone number comes from a partnership with Hushed instead: a free international virtual number set up inside the AloSIM app after you buy any plan, no ID verification required. It's genuinely useful for receiving a verification text or giving a hotel a number that isn't your real one, but it's a second line for texts and app-based calls, not a real local number that connects to the regular phone network the way ByteSIM's does.
  </p>

