  <h2>Is ByteSIM or AloSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with ByteSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You need a number a local bank, delivery service, or landlord can actually call or text</li>
        <li>Your destination is one of ByteSIM's supported local-number countries</li>
        <li>You don't mind uploading ID to get a real number</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with AloSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You just need a number for verification texts or to avoid handing out your real one</li>
        <li>You'd rather not do ID verification at all</li>
        <li>You want the option of a fixed-GB or unlimited plan, not just number-bundled ones</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>ByteSIM's number plans bill on your terms.</strong> Per ByteSIM's own support, a plan doesn't start counting against your data or minutes until you actually tap activate, even after ID verification is done, so there's no rush once you've installed it. AloSIM works the same way on the refund side instead: it advertises a no-questions refund if an eSIM won't connect or the device turns out incompatible, as long as you haven't put real use on it yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Support style differs too.</strong> ByteSIM handles support over WhatsApp with a real person, including proactively checking back in on activation issues rather than waiting for another message. AloSIM's refund policy covers the same kind of activation problem from a different angle: instead of a support conversation, you get your money back.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>ByteSIM vs AloSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which gives you a more useful phone number, ByteSIM or AloSIM</h3>
      <p>Depends on the use case. ByteSIM's number is real and connects like a normal local number, but only in its supported countries and only after ID verification. AloSIM's Hushed number is free and needs no ID, but it's a virtual second line rather than a full local number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do I have to verify my identity with either provider</h3>
      <p>Only with ByteSIM, and only if you buy a plan that includes a local phone number. AloSIM requires no ID verification for its Hushed number or for standard data plans.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper</h3>
      <p>They're not really comparable at the number level: ByteSIM bundles local calls and a large data allowance into its number plans, while AloSIM's number rides free on a plain data plan. Compare the specific plan you'd actually buy on each site.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>What if the eSIM doesn't work</h3>
      <p>AloSIM advertises a straightforward refund for eSIMs that fail to connect or are incompatible with the device. ByteSIM leans on responsive WhatsApp support instead, and its plans don't start billing until you tap activate, so there's less pressure if activation is delayed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

